m enforces the reliance.\n    </md>\n  </concept>\n\n  <concept id="architectural-quantum" state="active" lineage="dehghani">\n    <title>Architectural quantum</title>\n    <fires-when embed="true">Drawing deployment or ownership boundaries; deciding what "one unit" of a system is.</fires-when>\n    <md embed="true">\nThe smallest independently deployable unit with high functional cohesion,\ncarrying everything its function needs: logic, contract, metadata,\ninfrastructure declaration. "Independent" means an independent lifecycle and\nownership — not necessarily isolated runtime. In socom, the promise is the\nquantum of work; in data platforms, the data product is the quantum of\narchitecture; both compose into aggregates without surrendering autonomy.\n    </md>\n  </concept>\n\n  <concept id="published-gate" state="active" lineage="akili">\n    <title>The published gate — safe-by-construction at the right altitude</title>\n    <fires-when embed="true">Designing any validation; deciding WHERE a failure should surface; consumer-facing errors that trace to producer omissions.</fires-when>\n    <md embed="true">\nMove validation to the altitude where the person who can fix it meets it: a\nproduct missing classification fails at the owner\'s publish step with an\nactionable message — never as a consumer\'s read-time 500. By the time anything\nis visible to a consumer it is safe-to-share by construction. Generalized:\nevery gate should fire at the desk of whoever owns the fix, as early as that\ndesk allows (shift-left applied to governance).\n    </md>\n  </concept>\n\n  <concept id="fail-posture" state="active" lineage="oreilly+akili">\n    <title>Fail-closed vs fail-open — choose the posture per concern</title>\n    <fires-when embed="true">Designing any failure path, fallback, degradation, or health check.</fires-when>\n    <md embed="true">\nDegradation is designed, never discovered. Security concerns (auth, masking,\nclassification) fail CLOSED: refuse rather than degrade, a 404 over an\nunmasked row. Quality concerns (freshness, completeness) fail OPEN: serve the\nprevious good version with degradation metadata rather than nothing. Every\nexternal dependency names its fallback at design time; a dependency without a\ndeclared posture is an undesigned outage.\n    </md>\n  </concept>\n\n  <concept id="falsifiable-success" state="active" lineage="akili">\n    <title>Falsifiable success metrics</title>\n    <fires-when embed="true">Writing any goal, contract, exit criterion, or "definition of done".</fires-when>\n    <md embed="true">\n"How will we know?" must answer with a command and an expected output — "when\n[command] returns [result]" — never with "looks good" or "feels right". If the\nsuccess of work cannot be falsified, the work cannot be promised, assessed, or\nhanded off; reframe until it can. This is the doctrine that makes every other\ngate possible.\n    </md>\n  </concept>\n\n</doctrine>\n',
