    'schemas/context.xml': '<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>\n<!--\n  socom context envelope — the CTX-1 artifact. One envelope per PROMISE\n  EXECUTION declares the context that work unit consumed: a seat ran a promise,\n  it read some input context, and it had a declared budget for that input. The\n  envelope is the durable, schema-valid record of that fact — context made a\n  first-class SOCOM artifact, so it can be gated and replayed instead of\n  vanishing with the process (verify-never-claim applied to context itself).\n\n  WHY A STANDALONE RECORD (not a <context> block inside schemas/promise.xml):\n  the promise is a frozen AGREEMENT — verbatim intent + a ratified contract the\n  promiser cannot amend. Context is the opposite kind of thing: a MUTABLE,\n  execution-time MEASUREMENT (what input was actually consumed vs. the declared\n  budget), produced per-attempt by the running seat. Embedding a mutable\n  execution measurement inside the immutable agreement conflates two lifecycles\n  in one mechanism and lets the promiser write into the record that carries the\n  contract binding it (constitution §separation-of-privilege). The run ledger is\n  the precedent: it stood up its own record (schemas/ledger.xml) rather than\n  bolting outcomes onto the promise. Context is execution-shaped, so it goes the\n  ledger\'s way — its own schema, its own .socom/context/ files.\n\n  STORAGE — one XML file per envelope at .socom/context/<id>.xml. XML not JSONL\n  (the ledger\'s format) because an envelope is written ONCE per work unit by a\n  single author: it is low-volume and structured, like a promise/handoff, and so\n  inherits the promise\'s rationale, not the ledger\'s high-volume flat-append one\n  (cf. schemas/ledger.xml lines 11-14). XML also earns tests/xmlcheck.py\n  well-formedness, `socom index` retrieval, and embed="true" for free.\n\n  This document is the schema-by-example: the canonical field contract (parsed,\n  single-sourced — `socom context verify` reads the <fields>/<invariant> here and\n  hardcodes nothing) plus a worked instance. The schema wrapper is\n  <context-schema>; a real envelope\'s root element is <context> (the artifact\n  legitimately owns that tag — unlike the ledger, whose rows are JSONL, leaving\n  <ledger> free as the wrapper).\n\n  Example envelope file (.socom/context/CTX-2026-0620-001.xml), verbatim:\n  <?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>\n  <context socom="0.1" id="CTX-2026-0620-001" promise="P-2026-0612-001"\n           seat="builder" ts="2026-06-20T12:00:00Z"\n           budget_tokens="8000" input_tokens="3200"/>\n-->\n<context-schema socom="0.1">\n\n  <!-- Field contract — these names ARE the envelope\'s root-element attributes. -->\n  <fields>\n    <field name="id"            type="id"        required="true">The envelope id (CTX-…). One per promise execution.</field>\n    <field name="promise"       type="id"        required="true">The promise this context belongs to (P-…) — the work unit (schemas/promise.xml).</field>\n    <field name="seat"          type="string"    required="true">The seat that emitted the envelope — builder, reviewer, scout, … (schemas/role.xml).</field>\n    <field name="ts"            type="iso-8601"  required="true">When the envelope was emitted (UTC).</field>\n    <field name="budget_tokens" type="int"       required="true">The DECLARED input-context budget for this work unit.</field>\n    <field name="input_tokens"  type="int"       required="true">The MEASURED input context actually consumed.</field>\n  </fields>\n\n  <!-- Cross-field invariants — evaluated over the int fields above. This is the\n       budget half of the CTX metric, declared here in exactly one place so the\n       verifier reads it rather than hardcoding the rule. op is one of\n       <= < >= > == ; lhs and rhs name int fields. -->\n  <invariants>\n    <invariant lhs="input_tokens" op="&lt;=" rhs="budget_tokens">\n      Measured input context must not exceed the declared budget — an\n      over-budget work unit is the ungoverned-context failure CTX exists to catch.\n    </invariant>\n    <!-- Lower bounds. rhs is an int LITERAL (not a field), evaluated by the same\n         invariant machinery: a negative token count is nonsense and, left\n         unchecked, a negative input_tokens trivially satisfies input <= budget\n         (a false PASS the CTX-1 reviewer flagged). -->\n    <invariant lhs="input_tokens" op="&gt;=" rhs="0">\n      Measured input context cannot be negative.\n    </invariant>\n    <invariant lhs="budget_tokens" op="&gt;=" rhs="0">\n      A declared budget cannot be negative.\n    </invariant>\n  </invariants>\n\n  <!-- Measurement contract (CTX-2) — how input_tokens is counted, declared in\n       ONE place so `socom context measure` and `socom context verify` both read\n       the divisor instead of hardcoding it (single source, as with the invariant\n       op above). estimator chars/N: tokens = round(len(text) / divisor). This is\n       the deterministic, offline, dependency-free "~4 chars per token" rule of\n       thumb (OpenAI) — NOT a model-exact tokenizer. It UNDER-counts code and\n       structured text, so it is a lower-bound estimate: leave budget headroom. A\n       BPE tokenizer for model-exact counts is deferred (CTX-3), behind the same\n       verb and this same element. -->\n  <measurement estimator="chars/4" divisor="4"/>\n\n  <!-- <inputs> — the per-component breakdown (CTX-2, LOAD-BEARING when present).\n       Each <input> names a readable repo-relative path and its measured token\n       cost. The OPTIONAL element keeps CTX-1 envelopes (no <inputs>) valid — they\n       fall back to the declared-vs-budget check only. But WHEN PRESENT, verify\n       re-measures every ref from the live artifact and FAILS unless:\n         (1) every ref resolves and is readable (degrade loudly, R6);\n         (2) each <input tokens> equals its ref\'s re-measure;\n         (3) the top-level input_tokens equals the sum of the re-measures.\n       This makes input_tokens un-forgeable: the gate recomputes it from the real\n       files, so a hand-authored count cannot pass. `socom context measure` writes\n       these counts; `socom context compress` drops the lowest-relevance inputs\n       (BM25/overlap-ranked vs the promise goal) until the sum fits the budget.\n       In CTX-2.1 ref is a repo-relative PATH; id-only refs are CTX-3. -->\n  <inputs>\n    <input ref="schemas/promise.xml" tokens="1400"/>\n    <input ref="canon/constitution.xml" tokens="1800"/>\n  </inputs>\n\n  <!-- Worked example: the readable contract. The live form is the standalone\n       file above (root <context>); this is the schema\'s documentation copy. A\n       fully-measured CTX-2 envelope carries <inputs> whose token sum equals\n       input_tokens; a CTX-1 envelope omits <inputs> and declares input_tokens. -->\n  <example>\n    <context socom="0.1" id="CTX-2026-0620-001" promise="P-2026-0612-001"\n             seat="builder" ts="2026-06-20T12:00:00Z"\n             budget_tokens="8000" input_tokens="3200">\n      <inputs>\n        <input ref="schemas/promise.xml" tokens="1400"/>\n        <input ref="canon/constitution.xml" tokens="1800"/>\n      </inputs>\n    </context>\n  </example>\n\n</context-schema>\n',
    'schemas/contract.xml': '<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>\n<!--\n  socom contract — the validation contract: falsifiable acceptance checks with\n  named assessors, written and ratified BEFORE implementation (constitution\n  §contracts-before-code). A promise (schemas/promise.xml) embeds one inline or\n  references one by `ref`; this schema documents that artifact as a first-class\n  shape so it can be made TESTABLE rather than inert.\n\n  WHY — "Tests confirm contracts; they are never retrofitted to bless whatever\n  was built." A contract whose checks only sit in XML is a claim, not a test.\n  `socom contract verify <file>` RUNS each check that carries a <run> command and\n  reports PASS/FAIL on its exit code — the SOCOM-native form of Akili\'s\n  conformance-test runner (contracts/tests/ + the contract:conformance CI gate),\n  collapsed to one verb over the contract we already write.\n\n  ASSESSOR MODEL — each <check> names the assessor that may judge it. A check\n  with a <run> is MECHANICALLY assessable (assessor="gate:*"): verify executes\n  it. A check WITHOUT a <run> (assessor="reviewer"/"human") is MANUAL: verify\n  reports it as needing that assessor and NEVER auto-passes it (fail-closed —\n  the mechanical assessor does not claim a human\'s verdict, §context-economy:\n  assessment is never delegated to the promiser, nor faked by the runner).\n\n  Example standalone contract (the readable form a promise embeds or refs):\n  the auto check runs `true` (exit 0 -> PASS); the manual check has no <run>.\n-->\n<contract socom="0.1">\n\n  <!-- Field contract — the elements/attributes a contract artifact carries. -->\n  <fields>\n    <field name="ref"               type="id"     required="true">C-&lt;date&gt;-&lt;nnn&gt;. The contract id; a promise points at it via &lt;contract ref="…"&gt;.</field>\n    <field name="state"             type="enum"   required="true" values="open|ratified|broken|renegotiated">open = drafted, not yet ratified; ratified = accepted before code (the only state work may start against); broken = a check falsified mid-build; renegotiated = superseded by a new version via the originator (a worker cannot amend its own — §contracts-before-code).</field>\n    <field name="goal"              type="string" required="true">The falsifiable definition of done, embed="true". What must be true, not how.</field>\n    <field name="check"             type="element" required="true">One acceptance check; repeatable. Attributes: id, assessor. Children: optional &lt;run&gt; (the command — its exit code is the verdict, 0=PASS), required &lt;expect&gt; (the outcome the assessor compares against). A check with a &lt;run&gt; is auto-verifiable; one without is MANUAL.</field>\n    <field name="out-of-scope"      type="string" required="false">What this contract deliberately does NOT cover, embed="true" — bounds the promise.</field>\n    <field name="regression-surface" type="string" required="false">What must keep working, embed="true" — the blast radius a fix must not break.</field>\n  </fields>\n\n  <!-- Worked example: a standalone contract. `socom contract verify` on this\n       reports check 1 PASS (true -> exit 0), check 2 MANUAL (no <run>), exit 0. -->\n  <example>\n    <contract ref="C-2026-0614-002" state="ratified">\n      <goal embed="true">\n        `socom contract verify` runs a contract\'s checks and exits nonzero iff an\n        auto check fails; manual checks are reported, never auto-passed.\n      </goal>\n      <check id="1" assessor="gate:task-completion">\n        <run>true</run>\n        <expect>exit 0 — the auto check passes and is reported PASS</expect>\n      </check>\n      <check id="2" assessor="reviewer">\n        <expect>verify prints this as MANUAL with assessor=reviewer; never auto-passed</expect>\n      </check>\n      <out-of-scope embed="true">\n        Comparing a check\'s live output against the prose &lt;expect&gt; — verify\n        runs the command and judges on exit code; the assessor reads the rest.\n      </out-of-scope>\n      <regression-surface embed="true">\n        A contract embedded in a promise must verify identically to a standalone one.\n      </regression-surface>\n    </contract>\n  </example>\n\n</contract>\n',
