    'schemas/role.xml': '<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>\n<!--\n  socom role — a SEAT, not a model. Defined by substrate operations: what it\n  reads, what it may write, what it promises, what it must never do. Any\n  model, runtime, or human can occupy it; trust accrues to the seat binding.\n-->\n<role id="R-reviewer" name="reviewer" version="1">\n\n  <description embed="true">\n    Independent adversarial assessor. Judges a builder\'s promise against the\n    ratified contract and the repo\'s conventions, from clean context. Its\n    promise is honest assessment — not approval.\n  </description>\n\n  <promises>\n    <promise embed="true">a verdict (approve/reject with findings) for every assigned promise, judged only against contract + conventions</promise>\n    <promise embed="true">findings are reproducible: each cites file, line, and the check that exposes it</promise>\n  </promises>\n\n  <substrate-operations>\n    <reads>diff under review, ratified contract, constitution, domain lessons</reads>\n    <writes>assessment elements appended to the promise artifact</writes>\n  </substrate-operations>\n\n  <never>\n    <rule>shares working context with the builder it assesses</rule>\n    <rule>edits the code under review</rule>\n    <rule>accepts the builder\'s own verification as evidence</rule>\n  </never>\n\n  <dispatch>\n    <trigger>every code-bearing promise before its state may become kept</trigger>\n    <preference>different model family than the builder (uncorrelated blind spots)</preference>\n    <brief>opens with USER VERBATIM of the originating intent (verbatim protocol)</brief>\n  </dispatch>\n\n  <trust seat-binding="per socom.yaml">\n    <metric>rolling kept/overturned assessment ratio; overturned = human reversed the verdict</metric>\n    <effect>low trust adds a second reviewer; high trust permits batch assessment</effect>\n  </trust>\n</role>\n',
    'schemas/run.xml': '<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>\n<!--\n  socom run — a launched worker, made a first-class, reapable artifact. One\n  record per SPAWN: a seat was dispatched into a promise against a contract,\n  with a brief forged in verbatim-protocol order. spawn WRITES the record;\n  monarch RECONCILES it (tally by liveness, reap a dead-but-running record into\n  a single amber breach). The split mirrors a track/supervise daemon lifecycle:\n  launch is one command, supervision another.\n\n  LOAD-BEARING BOUNDARY — a run record carries LIVENESS and PROVENANCE, never\n  JUDGMENT. spawn and the spawned worker never write a kept-or-broken verdict;\n  only the gate, contract verify with record, or a monarch reap may (verify-\n  never-claim, separation-of-privilege). status here is lifecycle, not outcome.\n\n  STORAGE — each run is its own JSON object on disk under the runs directory\n  (.socom/runs), named by the run id with a .json suffix; the forged brief sits\n  beside it with a .brief.md suffix, and a captured launch log with a .log\n  suffix when exec-launched. One file per run (not an append-only log) because a\n  run is mutated in place by reconcile: status flips materialized to running to\n  done-or-dead, and pid plus the ended fields fill in. Writes are atomic — a\n  sibling temp file is renamed over the target — and serialized across spawners\n  by one advisory lock in the runs directory, the same POSIX locking the run\n  ledger uses. In-repo and git-propagated, so a teammate sees open runs.\n\n  IDENTITY — the run id is content-addressed off the brief bytes:\n  R-YEAR-MONTHDAY-SEAT-HASH8, where HASH8 is the first 8 hex of the SHA-256 of\n  the brief. The brief excludes any timestamp or id, so identical intent yields\n  identical bytes and therefore an idempotent id: re-spawning the same promise\n  into the same seat resolves the same record. This mirrors the content-address\n  the next-session prompt id already uses.\n\n  Example record (the 